(EXP-51: `if x, y, and z are true` expands to
 `if x is true and y is true and z is true` - the plural form and the written-
 out conjunction agree on every combination.
 Both forms are asked the same question three times, with a different subject
 flipped each time, so an expansion that dropped or duplicated a subject would
 show up as a disagreement rather than as a coincidence.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox EXP-51.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   plural TTT
   written TTT
   plural FTT false
   written FTT false
   plural TFT false
   written TFT false
   plural TTF false
   written TTF false
)
a boolean called armed is true.
a boolean called latched is true.
a boolean called sealed is true.
If armed, latched, and sealed are true then, Print "plural TTT". Otherwise, Print "plural TTT false".
If armed is true and latched is true and sealed is true then, Print "written TTT". Otherwise, Print "written TTT false".
Set armed to false.
If armed, latched, and sealed are true then, Print "plural FTT". Otherwise, Print "plural FTT false".
If armed is true and latched is true and sealed is true then, Print "written FTT". Otherwise, Print "written FTT false".
Set armed to true.
Set latched to false.
If armed, latched, and sealed are true then, Print "plural TFT". Otherwise, Print "plural TFT false".
If armed is true and latched is true and sealed is true then, Print "written TFT". Otherwise, Print "written TFT false".
Set latched to true.
Set sealed to false.
If armed, latched, and sealed are true then, Print "plural TTF". Otherwise, Print "plural TTF false".
If armed is true and latched is true and sealed is true then, Print "written TTF". Otherwise, Print "written TTF false".
